WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.188
MIKE COLEMAN: Hi.

00:00:01.188 --> 00:00:04.170
I'm Mike Coleman, a Developer
Advocate here at Google Cloud.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.670
In this video, I
want to walk you

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.790
through how you can use Google
Cloud's Migrate for Anthos

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.360
to move existing Linux
workloads from virtual machines

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.210
to containers in
just a few steps.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.190
This video builds
on an earlier one,

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.990
Introducing Migrate for Anthos.

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.730
If you haven't watched
that video yet,

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:22.920
definitely go check
it out, as this one

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.370
assumes you've performed the
prerequisites we outline there.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.740
Building on what you learned
in the first video, let's

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.840
jump into an actual migration.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.850
As a brief recap,
Migrate for Anthos

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.370
has three primary components.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.770
The discovery tool
assesses how successful

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.590
a migration of a given
application might be.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.050
The processing cluster
is a Kubernetes cluster

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.300
that is used to
examine the source VM

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.760
and create the migration
plan, as well as execute

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.110
the actual migration.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.840
And finally, migctl,
a command line tool,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.140
like kubectl, for interacting
with the processing cluster.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.140
We'll do most of our work today
in the Google Cloud Console,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.570
but I will show you
a bit of migctl.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.060
And again, to quickly
recap, a migration

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.770
generally consists of
the following steps.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.680
First, create a
Google Cloud project,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.510
set up the necessary
service accounts,

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.030
and enable the appropriate APIs.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.760
Then you create a migration
processing cluster.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.280
After that, choose and
qualify your target workload.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.220
Then you define your
migration source,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.160
create a migration plan,
and adjust that plan

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.230
to ensure it meets your needs.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.470
After that, you create the
actual deployment artifacts.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.990
And then finally, you can
deploy the newly containerized

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.220
application.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.960
The first two steps were
covered in the previous video,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.947
so we'll start by discussing
choosing the right workload.

00:01:37.947 --> 00:01:40.530
There are some applications that
are well-suited for migration

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.030
and some that are not.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.730
Linux applications that are a
good fit for migration using

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.950
Migrate for Anthos include
the following application

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.560
architectures-- web and
application servers,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.530
business logic middleware--
for example, Tomcat--

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.580
multi-VM, multi-tier
stacks-- for example, lamp--

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.560
small and medium
sized databases,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.480
like MySQL and PostgreSQL.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.580
In addition,
applications best suited

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.200
for migration with
Migrate for Anthos

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.630
have the following
operational characteristics.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.080
They have a low duty-cycle
and bursty workloads,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.590
they're always-on with low
load services, or deployment,

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.050
testing, and training-lab
environments.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.672
In general, most Linux workloads
are compatible with migration.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:21.630
However, there are some
types of workloads that

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:23.160
are not really recommended.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.130
These would include high
performance and large in-memory

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:30.240
databases, VMs with special
kernel drivers-- for example,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.190
kernel-mode NFS--

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.980
apps with dependencies
on specific hardware,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.370
and software with licenses
tied to certain hardware ID

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:39.600
registration.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.660
For this demo, I'm using a
Java application housed in a VM

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.170
which also has a
Postgres database.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.145
So I have a good feeling
that this application

00:02:48.145 --> 00:02:49.770
is an appropriate
candidate to migrate.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.690
But how can I be sure?

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.460
Well, we have a
utility for that--

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.920
the Migrate for
Anthos discovery tool.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.300
The discovery tool
has two parts.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.260
The first is a collection script
that runs on the source VM

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.400
and analyzes the
running application.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.850
The analysis script then looks
at that information gathered

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:11.120
from the collection phase and
gives us a score of 1 to 10

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.760
on the likelihood of a
successful migration.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.550
You can run your analysis
script on a centralized server,

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:19.592
allowing you to batch
process collected data.

00:03:19.592 --> 00:03:21.800
So let's jump into our demo
and look at the discovery

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.217
tool, which I've
already installed

00:03:23.217 --> 00:03:25.640
on the source VM running
on Google Compute Engine.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:27.590
You can consult the
Migrate for Anthos docs

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.780
for info on how to
install the tool.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.440
Before we look at
the discovery tool,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.920
I wanted to take a quick
overview of the sample

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.080
application we're working with,
which is the Cymbal Bank app.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.225
So I'm going to go ahead, come
down here, and click Sign In.

00:03:40.225 --> 00:03:41.600
And what you're
going to see here

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.820
is a typical
banking application.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.310
We're looking at the
ledger view here.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:48.650
I can come up and I
can click Send Payment.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.895
I can choose another
user from the dropdown.

00:03:50.895 --> 00:03:51.770
We'll use Alice here.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.860
I'm going to send her $100,
and we're going to click Send.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.630
And you can see
the ledger update.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:58.790
So everything is working
the way we expect it to.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:00.770
Let's look a little bit
at how that application

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.380
is deployed on the back end.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:04.970
The application is
actually made up

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:07.310
of a combination of
Kubernetes services

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.140
and then a monolithic service
running in a virtual machine.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.010
So on the Kubernetes side, we
have four services running.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.100
They handle everything from
managing the accounts database

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.079
to the web front end
we just interacted

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:20.269
with to that dropdown
list that we saw,

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:23.150
where we can pull up
associated accounts

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.450
that the main user has.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:26.590
We come into here.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:28.510
This is our VM that
we're going to migrate,

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:29.760
and that's the ledger service.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.560
So everything you saw there
that dealt with the ledger, that

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.140
is handled here-- so how
do we update the ledger,

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.770
check balances,
those sort of things.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:40.060
The other thing is that this
monolith includes the database,

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.230
the ledger database.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.660
So there's an accounts
database and a ledger database,

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:46.520
and the ledger database
lives inside of this VM.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.450
So when we're done, we're
going to have migrated this VM

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.010
into a Kubernetes service.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.970
So let's get started with that.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:53.650
So here I am.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:57.065
I've SSH'd into that
ledger monolith service VM.

00:04:57.065 --> 00:04:58.690
And as I mentioned
before, I've already

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.190
installed the Migrate for
Anthos discovery tool,

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:02.620
so what we want to do
here is, let's take a look

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:03.787
at what that's comprised of.

00:05:03.787 --> 00:05:05.988
You have the collect script
and the analysis script.

00:05:05.988 --> 00:05:08.530
So we're going to go ahead and
run that collect script first.

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:12.160
So let me run that,
and that's going

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.700
to produce a TAR file here.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.870
And we're going to go ahead
and then run the analysis

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:19.030
script against that TAR file.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:20.680
So we'll go ahead and run that.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.450
And then that gives us
a CSV file, and let's

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.570
cat out the contents
of that file.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.880
So this is a little hard
to read on the screen.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.090
So what I've done is, I've
actually taken and moved

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.550
that CSV onto my local
machine and loaded it up

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:42.140
into a Google Sheet.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:44.002
There's a lot of
information here.

00:05:44.002 --> 00:05:45.960
I'm only going to point
out a couple of things.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.010
First, our fit score is a
9, and that is right here.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.620
So we've got a 9
out of 10, and that

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:55.280
means it's a great fit with
none to very little manual work,

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.730
based on the glossary here.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:58.188
The other thing I
want to point out

00:05:58.188 --> 00:06:00.800
is that we do have a
Postgres database in the VM

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.197
that the fit tool
discovered, and we'll

00:06:03.197 --> 00:06:05.030
have to deal with that
during our migration.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.207
But with a score of 9, we
should feel pretty good

00:06:07.207 --> 00:06:09.290
that we should be able to
migrate this application

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.040
with very little effort.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.790
So the next step is to actually
create a migration source.

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.370
So we're going to do that by
moving back here in the Cloud

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:20.300
Console, and we're going
to go to Anthos, Migrate

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.290
to containers.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.540
Remember, we've already set
up our processing cluster.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.380
We did that in our last video.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.827
So the processing
cluster is right here.

00:06:27.827 --> 00:06:29.410
So now we're going
to create a source.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:31.610
So we'll come in and
we'll say Add Source.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:34.130
And we're going to select
our processing cluster.

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:35.270
Click Next.

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.760
We're going to go and
give this source a name.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.920
And it's going to come
from Compute Engine.

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.100
Remember, it can come from
VMware or AWS or Azure,

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:45.440
as well, but in this case,
we're using Compute Engine.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.420
We'll click Next.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.430
In the first video,
we created one service

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.650
account to actually let
us install the components

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:52.853
on the processing cluster.

00:06:52.853 --> 00:06:54.770
And now we're going to
create a second service

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:57.350
account that lets us deal
with that Compute Engine

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:58.050
as a source.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.100
So we will put the Compute
Viewer and the Compute Storage

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.830
Admin roles onto
the service account.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:08.735
We'll click Next, and we'll
go ahead and add the source.

00:07:08.735 --> 00:07:09.610
So we come back here.

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.670
We have our source all
ready for us to go.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:15.195
Next, let's move on to
create our migration.

00:07:15.195 --> 00:07:17.320
Now one thing to note is
that before I can actually

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:20.030
migrate a VM, I have to stop
it, which I've already done.

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:22.360
I've gone into the
Google Cloud Console

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.700
and I've stopped that
ledger service VM,

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.410
so it's not running any longer.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:28.030
With that, I would
come in here now

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.190
and I click Create Migration.

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:34.420
I take the migration name,
and I'll give it a name here.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.640
We'll come down and
we'll select the source

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.610
that we just created,
Linux operating system.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:45.700
And the virtual machine ID
is ledgermonolith-service, so

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:46.750
right there.

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:50.560
And we're going to migrate
both the image and data.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:55.090
In this case, that monolith
VM has Postgres running in it,

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:58.820
so we need both the
application code and the data.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.180
So let's click Create Migration.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.660
This will take a
couple of minutes,

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:04.327
so I'll fast forward
the video, and I'll

00:08:04.327 --> 00:08:06.490
rejoin you when it's done.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.270
Our migration plan
has been generated.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.330
So the next step here
is now to make sure

00:08:10.330 --> 00:08:13.005
that migration plan is accurate
for what we want to do.

00:08:13.005 --> 00:08:14.380
So we can come
here into Options,

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:16.990
and we can Review and
edit the migration plan.

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:19.150
Now I know one thing I'm
going to need to change

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.820
is, down here
under data volumes,

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:26.230
I need to go ahead and tell it
that I want to use the Postgres

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:27.190
folder, right?

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:29.770
So we're going to point it
at that Postres subdirectory,

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:33.159
because remember, we have
that database running there

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:34.970
in our monolithic VM.

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:38.440
So with that, I can save this
and generate my artifacts.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.750
So let's click Save
and Generate Artifacts.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:43.659
And it'll save it, and it'll
start generating the artifacts.

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:45.555
Again, this will take
a couple of minutes,

00:08:45.555 --> 00:08:46.930
so I'll pause the
video, and I'll

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.000
rejoin you when it finishes.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.220
So the migration has
completed, and our artifacts

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:52.090
have been generated.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:53.980
I can click on the
link of the migration

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.500
here, and click on
the Artifacts tab,

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:58.100
and you can get a
list of all of them.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:01.420
So we have a Docker file, but
the Docker image has actually

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.190
already been
created and uploaded

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:04.930
to Container Registry
for us, so we don't

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:06.520
need to do anything with that.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.770
What we really care about
is this deployment spec.

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:10.270
Now there's a couple
of ways you can

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:12.280
grab the deployment spec
or any of the artifacts

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:13.130
for that matter.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:15.820
One is they're stored
in a storage bucket

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:17.990
up on Google Cloud
Storage, and you

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.240
could download them from here.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.660
The other way to do it is to
use migctl, the command line

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:24.850
utility, and that's
what we're going to do.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:27.310
So I'm going to move
over into Cloud Shell,

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:31.150
and I'm going to issue a
migctl migration get-artifacts

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:35.000
command, and that's going to
download the artifacts for me.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.940
So let me go ahead
here and I'll list out

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:40.270
the deployment_spec.yaml, just
to verify it got downloaded.

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:42.370
And that's a standard
Kubernetes yaml

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.590
file for deploying the
service and some other stuff.

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:46.550
We're not going to spend a
lot of time going into that.

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:48.940
But what we are going to do
now is actually apply it.

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:52.090
Now the processing cluster is
different from the production

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:53.710
cluster where I run
my applications.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:55.360
So I need to
actually authenticate

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:56.810
into that production cluster.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:09:59.590
So let me go ahead and
issue the gcloud command

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:01.660
to authenticate into
my production cluster,

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:03.790
and we will list out
the nodes, just to make

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.840
sure I have the right cluster.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:07.830
And I do.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:13.020
So the next step is to actually
just do a kubectl minus f apply

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:16.030
and apply the deployment spec.

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:17.860
And that's going to
create a stateful set.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.380
It's going to create a
service, a Persistent Volume,

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:22.060
and a Persistent Volume Claim.

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:24.340
The Persistent Volume and
the Persistent Volume Claim

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:27.190
will be used to handle that
Postgres database we migrated

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.680
over from our monolith VM.

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:32.290
The next thing we need to do
is ensure all of the services

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:33.650
can find each other.

00:10:33.650 --> 00:10:35.650
The Cymbal Bank app
does this through a set

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:38.680
of configuration variables.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.290
Currently, the variables
associated with the ledger

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:44.200
service are pointing to the
Compute Engine internal DNS

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.230
name of the ledger
monolith VM, which

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:49.690
has the format of
instance name dot

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:54.010
c dot project ID dot internal,
which you can see right here.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:56.740
I'm going to swap those out
for the Kubernetes service

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:00.590
name, which is simply
ledgermonolith-service.

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:02.418
And if I list my
Kubernetes services,

00:11:02.418 --> 00:11:04.210
you can see that service
listed right here.

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:10.240
So the next thing to do is
actually apply that config map,

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.940
so those new service
mappings take place.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.460
So we'll apply the
config map here.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.510
Next, let's go ahead and
delete all the existing pods,

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:22.320
and that will force them to
pick up the new mappings when

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.270
they regenerate.

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:25.890
And after that, what
we're going to do

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:27.520
is we're going to
list our services,

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:31.140
so we can grab the IP
address of our front end,

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.270
and check out and make
sure the application

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:34.660
is still working as expected.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:36.240
So I'll copy this.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.590
I'm going to paste it
up here into my browser.

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:40.290
So the Cymbal Bank,
the app has loaded.

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:42.270
Let's sign in again.

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:43.080
So we're signed in.

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:43.830
Here's the ledger.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.750
Let's go and do a send
payment to Alice again.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:50.197
Let's give her $250 this
time, and we'll click Send.

00:11:50.197 --> 00:11:51.780
And we can see, the
ledger is updated.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.640
So our application
is working exactly

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.400
as we expect it to after
a successful migration.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:57.430
So there you have it.

